+# Fixture: python_shadowed_owner
+
+Spec: RIPR-SPEC-0028; issue #8247.
+
+## Given
+
+The imported production function changes `amount * 2` to `amount * 3`, while
+the test replaces its local `fee` with `Mock(return_value=30)`. Both production
+versions pass that test: the exact assertion reads the substitute's fixed
+payload.
+
+## When
+
+`ripr check` analyzes the changed return through the Python preview adapter.
+
+## Then
+
+The changed production return remains `weakly_exposed` under the existing
+syntactic relation, without a strong observation alignment.
+
+`corpus.json` retains all eleven independently authored literal cases from
+[the immutable evidence](https://github.com/EffortlessMetrics/ripr-swarm/blob/8e091dc8b6bfca3649cd8c778bffccdb905f160e/README.md).
+The five owner-shadow false credits must be refused, the exception-only
+control remains weak, and the five genuine output controls remain exposed.
+The nested helper control binds `fee` only in the helper's own scope: the
+outer assertion still calls the production owner. Do not reject it with a
+whole-body text scan. The retained native verifier also checks AssertionError
+for every expected old-value failure; no setup error is a behavioral witness.
+
+## Must Not
+
+Credit the imported owner from a substitute result, a different test row, or
+a same-named method on an unrelated receiver.
+
+The corpus labels are literal and independent of the analyzer. A justified
+relation-layer change to `no_static_path` requires an explicit contract
+revision; it cannot justify blessing a false `exposed` result.
